1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,830
Someone opens Telegram and sends one message:

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:07,070
please fix the broken signup link on the website.

3
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:13,120
This video follows that message through Chief by Templ, step by step, from the phone

4
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,550
to the project and back.

5
00:00:15,392 --> 00:00:20,112
First, what is Chief? The core is a set of instructions and a few Markdown files in

6
00:00:20,112 --> 00:00:23,462
a private folder. The agents file holds the rules.

7
00:00:23,552 --> 00:00:27,282
The projects file lists each project and its existing lead.

8
00:00:27,352 --> 00:00:29,962
The priorities file holds your priorities.

9
00:00:30,312 --> 00:00:34,302
Task cards live in six folders, one for each board column.

10
00:00:34,472 --> 00:00:38,982
There is no planning engine, no vector database and no model server.

11
00:00:39,112 --> 00:00:43,142
Your own Codex model reads these files and makes the decisions.

12
00:00:44,047 --> 00:00:47,097
Setup starts in the Codex app that you already use.

13
00:00:47,147 --> 00:00:50,477
You paste one line that points Codex at the Chief guide.

14
00:00:50,647 --> 00:00:52,737
Codex clones the public source.

15
00:00:52,787 --> 00:00:55,517
It installs Git only if Git is missing.

16
00:00:55,587 --> 00:00:59,737
Then it makes a separate private workspace, outside the public clone.

17
00:00:59,907 --> 00:01:02,857
You can choose a nickname, or keep the name Chief.

18
00:01:03,127 --> 00:01:06,437
Codex asks before it reads any project history.

19
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:11,107
It proposes your projects and their existing leads, and you accept the list or

20
00:01:11,107 --> 00:01:11,937
change it.

21
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:15,639
Telegram is an optional adapter, and it needs more.

22
00:01:15,669 --> 00:01:21,249
Python 3.12 or later. Three pinned libraries, for the Codex connection and for

23
00:01:21,249 --> 00:01:23,019
native Telegram formatting.

24
00:01:23,089 --> 00:01:26,779
The Codex command line tool, and a new bot from BotFather.

25
00:01:27,089 --> 00:01:29,099
Three setup commands do the work.

26
00:01:29,329 --> 00:01:31,999
Prepare copies the code and makes blank records.

27
00:01:32,369 --> 00:01:37,239
Connect checks the bot and creates one coordinator thread, without a model turn.

28
00:01:37,809 --> 00:01:41,079
Service installs one collector that starts at login.

29
00:01:41,629 --> 00:01:44,639
On Linux, you run the collector in the foreground.

30
00:01:45,623 --> 00:01:47,713
Authentication has three parts.

31
00:01:47,743 --> 00:01:51,373
Codex keeps its own sign-in, and Chief never copies it.

32
00:01:51,423 --> 00:01:56,483
The bot token goes in through a hidden prompt, into an owner-only file that Git

33
00:01:56,483 --> 00:02:02,113
ignores. Then you open a private invitation link in your own Telegram and tap Start.

34
00:02:02,223 --> 00:02:04,213
That enrolls your account.

35
00:02:04,503 --> 00:02:09,853
Messages from anyone else get no reply, no storage and no model access.

36
00:02:10,792 --> 00:02:15,682
Now the message arrives. The collector is a small Python process on your computer.

37
00:02:15,732 --> 00:02:18,402
It long-polls Telegram for new updates.

38
00:02:18,472 --> 00:02:21,332
While it waits, it makes no model calls.

39
00:02:21,332 --> 00:02:26,102
It checks the sender, and it refuses text that looks like a password or a key.

40
00:02:26,172 --> 00:02:31,812
Then it saves the request in a local SQLite database, with an ID made from the chat

41
00:02:31,812 --> 00:02:37,352
and message numbers. A saved request survives a restart, and the same update is not

42
00:02:37,352 --> 00:02:38,322
stored twice.

43
00:02:39,318 --> 00:02:41,538
Next, the adapter gives the request to Chief.

44
00:02:41,538 --> 00:02:45,798
It talks to the Codex app server on your computer, through a local socket.

45
00:02:45,798 --> 00:02:48,738
Chief is one continuing Codex thread.

46
00:02:48,738 --> 00:02:53,538
Setup created it once, and the adapter resumes the same thread every time.

47
00:02:53,538 --> 00:02:56,878
If Chief is idle, the adapter starts a new turn.

48
00:02:56,878 --> 00:03:00,058
If Chief is busy, it steers the active turn.

49
00:03:00,058 --> 00:03:04,078
The input says who sent the message and what authority they have.

50
00:03:04,078 --> 00:03:06,998
It adds the text and the part they replied to.

51
00:03:06,998 --> 00:03:12,348
The model itself runs in the model service behind your Codex account.

52
00:03:13,215 --> 00:03:17,975
Because the thread continues, a short message lands in a context that can already be

53
00:03:17,975 --> 00:03:23,965
large. Codex manages that context window, and it compacts the context when needed.

54
00:03:24,435 --> 00:03:29,185
Chief reads only the records it needs, but that does not erase earlier turns.

55
00:03:29,275 --> 00:03:32,385
So each turn still uses model capacity.

56
00:03:32,475 --> 00:03:35,905
Chief sets no token budget and no time limit.

57
00:03:36,598 --> 00:03:39,348
Chief now decides. It can answer directly.

58
00:03:39,378 --> 00:03:43,208
Or it can choose an existing project lead from the projects file.

59
00:03:43,218 --> 00:03:45,318
Here, it chooses the website lead.

60
00:03:45,318 --> 00:03:48,208
It sends one focused brief through its route tool.

61
00:03:48,318 --> 00:03:52,268
The adapter resumes that lead's own thread and starts a turn there.

62
00:03:52,278 --> 00:03:57,048
The lead keeps its own repository, tools, model settings and context.

63
00:03:57,278 --> 00:04:00,698
If the lead is busy in the Codex app, the brief waits.

64
00:04:00,698 --> 00:04:03,418
Chief does not create a replacement lead.

65
00:04:03,418 --> 00:04:05,948
This adapter routes to Codex leads.

66
00:04:06,338 --> 00:04:10,988
For a lead in another tool, Chief reports the gap and keeps that lead.

67
00:04:11,821 --> 00:04:15,151
The website lead fixes the link and runs its checks.

68
00:04:15,181 --> 00:04:19,551
When its turn completes, the adapter captures the final answer.

69
00:04:19,581 --> 00:04:23,181
That answer comes back to Chief as evidence from the lead.

70
00:04:23,181 --> 00:04:27,751
It is not a new instruction from you, and it grants no new authority.

71
00:04:28,605 --> 00:04:30,705
Chief records the result on the board.

72
00:04:30,705 --> 00:04:33,225
Each task is one Markdown file.

73
00:04:33,225 --> 00:04:34,995
Its folder is its status.

74
00:04:35,365 --> 00:04:38,835
Backlog holds ideas. Todo holds committed work.

75
00:04:38,985 --> 00:04:43,625
Doing holds active work. Blocked holds work that waits for a concrete missing input,

76
00:04:43,625 --> 00:04:45,395
and it names who can supply it.

77
00:04:45,585 --> 00:04:48,075
Review waits for a named person's decision.

78
00:04:48,485 --> 00:04:50,435
Done holds a verified outcome.

79
00:04:50,565 --> 00:04:53,345
A move takes the same file to a new folder.

80
00:04:53,345 --> 00:04:58,235
The move adds a line with the time, the old and new columns, and a reason.

81
00:04:58,905 --> 00:05:03,635
Here, the card goes from Doing to Review, because you must accept the fix.

82
00:05:04,457 --> 00:05:09,467
Then Chief replies. It calls the reply tool with the exact request ID.

83
00:05:09,637 --> 00:05:12,507
The reply goes into a local outbox.

84
00:05:12,557 --> 00:05:15,547
Chief's ordinary chat text is not sent.

85
00:05:15,637 --> 00:05:20,017
The collector splits a long answer to fit Telegram's message limit.

86
00:05:20,017 --> 00:05:23,527
It sends each part and saves a receipt for each one.

87
00:05:23,537 --> 00:05:27,747
If a send is uncertain, the collector does not repeat it blindly.

88
00:05:28,593 --> 00:05:31,403
At any time, you can send /report.

89
00:05:31,453 --> 00:05:35,133
The adapter reads the saved cards and sends the board.

90
00:05:35,133 --> 00:05:38,643
It makes no model call and no live project check.

91
00:05:39,441 --> 00:05:42,071
So, what runs where? On your computer:

92
00:05:42,081 --> 00:05:47,081
the Codex app and its app server, the collector, SQLite, the Markdown files, and the

93
00:05:47,081 --> 00:05:48,991
project checkouts with their tools.

94
00:05:49,121 --> 00:05:53,671
In the model service: every model turn, for Chief and for each lead.

95
00:05:53,941 --> 00:05:56,251
Telegram's servers carry the messages.

96
00:05:56,781 --> 00:05:59,691
Model turns use your existing Codex plan.

97
00:05:59,881 --> 00:06:03,511
Chief adds no model of its own, but it does use the model.

98
00:06:04,273 --> 00:06:07,473
Now, memory. Chief has no hidden memory store.

99
00:06:07,473 --> 00:06:10,183
It has layers, and each layer has a place.

100
00:06:10,233 --> 00:06:14,123
Your nickname goes in the coordinator instructions and the setup record.

101
00:06:14,333 --> 00:06:18,923
With Telegram, a private profile file also holds it, to name the bot.

102
00:06:19,053 --> 00:06:23,153
Your operating style and preferences go in the private instructions and the

103
00:06:23,153 --> 00:06:28,173
priorities file. Choices about devices and leads go in the projects file.

104
00:06:28,173 --> 00:06:32,563
Chief writes these records when you state a preference or accept a proposal.

105
00:06:32,993 --> 00:06:36,003
It does not learn every preference by itself.

106
00:06:36,961 --> 00:06:41,841
Runtime settings, such as the coordinator thread ID and the maintenance switch, stay

107
00:06:41,841 --> 00:06:44,051
in a private folder on your computer.

108
00:06:44,241 --> 00:06:49,391
Secrets stay there too: the bot token, the invitation and the message database.

109
00:06:49,861 --> 00:06:52,451
Codex keeps its own sign-in and sessions.

110
00:06:52,761 --> 00:06:56,971
Codex also keeps the thread context and its compaction summaries.

111
00:06:57,081 --> 00:07:02,181
If your Codex supplies its own memory, Chief treats it as a hint to check against

112
00:07:02,181 --> 00:07:03,331
the current files.

113
00:07:04,162 --> 00:07:06,052
That gives three kinds of files.

114
00:07:06,062 --> 00:07:10,312
Public source is the code and the guides, with no personal data.

115
00:07:10,362 --> 00:07:15,572
An optional private Git backup can hold your instructions, project list, priorities,

116
00:07:15,602 --> 00:07:18,112
cards, reports and cleaned chat exports.

117
00:07:18,382 --> 00:07:20,902
Local-only state never goes into Git:

118
00:07:20,902 --> 00:07:24,492
secrets, runtime settings, databases and sessions.

119
00:07:24,602 --> 00:07:27,142
A Git backup restores your records.

120
00:07:27,142 --> 00:07:30,472
It does not restore your sign-ins or pending deliveries.

121
00:07:31,443 --> 00:07:34,643
To sum up, the core is plain files and your Codex.

122
00:07:34,643 --> 00:07:37,833
It needs no Python and no SQLite.

123
00:07:37,883 --> 00:07:40,433
The Telegram adapter is optional.

124
00:07:40,463 --> 00:07:46,133
It adds sender checks, a durable inbox, delivery receipts and /report.

125
00:07:46,163 --> 00:07:49,093
It does not plan, and it does not rank models.

126
00:07:49,975 --> 00:07:52,345
One message. One continuing Chief.

127
00:07:52,375 --> 00:07:54,325
Your existing project leads.

128
00:07:54,395 --> 00:07:56,865
One private board. Chief, by Templ.
